{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1332 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2e439684647a6f2cd72fd2889885db359297b4e8d2822b6ae59839ce983009f5" :edb/db-before-t 1331 :edb/db-after-t 1332 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534644 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:04:42.742Z" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054355 1100 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}$?\n\n1. $\\{-2, 0, 4 \\}$\n2. $\\{0, 3 \\}$\n3. $\\{-3, 1, 3 \\}$" 13194139534644 false] [17592186054355 1100 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}?$\n\n- $\\{ -2, 0, 4 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ 0, 3 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ -3, 1, 3 \\}$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054355 1145 "First, we must find the elements of the set $S$. Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\le 2n + 1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4 \\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2 \\le n & \\le 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S$. With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{-2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\{0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- $\\{-3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534644 false] [17592186054355 1145 "First, we must find the elements of the set $S.$ Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2n+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4\\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2\\le n & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{ -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{ -2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\{ 0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $\\{ -3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534644 true] [17592186054356 1100 "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054356 1100 "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{a, b, c \\}$." 13194139534644 false] [17592186054356 1145 "To find every subset of $A$, we list out every combination of elements within $A$.\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/50/Source/Images/7bf948604295a59e82bc1c70608fce52.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}\n$$" 13194139534644 false] [17592186054356 1145 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054357 1100 "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054357 1100 "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n2. $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n3. $\\mathbb{N}$" 13194139534644 false] [17592186054357 1145 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534644 false] [17592186054357 1145 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054358 1100 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054358 1100 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}$?\n\n1. $A\\subseteq B$\n2. $B\\subseteq A$\n3. $B\\nsubseteq A$\n4. $B\\supseteq A$" 13194139534644 false] [17592186054358 1145 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B$.\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10$, that is, $n\\geq 5$. So, we have\n$A= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}$.\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5$, that is, $n\\geq 3$. So, we have\n$B= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}$.\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B$.\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A$.\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B$, which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139534644 false] [17592186054358 1145 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139534644 true] [17592186054542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300887 13194139534644 true] [17592186054542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300922 13194139534644 true] [17592186054542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300929 13194139534644 true] [17592186054542 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300943 13194139534644 true] [17592186054543 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300894 13194139534644 true] [17592186054543 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300964 13194139534644 true] [17592186054543 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300971 13194139534644 true] [17592186054543 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300978 13194139534644 true] [17592186054544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300901 13194139534644 true] [17592186054544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300908 13194139534644 true] [17592186054544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300957 13194139534644 true] [17592186054544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300985 13194139534644 true] [17592186054544 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300992 13194139534644 true] [17592186054545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300915 13194139534644 true] [17592186054545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300936 13194139534644 true] [17592186054545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300950 13194139534644 true] [17592186054545 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300999 13194139534644 true] [17592186054889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534644 true] [17592186054889 1100 "Find all of the subsets of $T = {1, 2, 3}$." 13194139534644 false] [17592186054889 1100 "Find all of the subsets of $T={1,2,3}.$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054889 1145 "To find every subset of $T,$ we list out every combination of elements within $T.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038640/assets/127ee1187ebf3a2cc498c88326285d5adf5d609cf3276885e8c9d525435133eb.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\,{1},\\,{2},\\,{3},\\,{1,2},\\,{1,3},\\,{2,3},\\,{1,2,3}.\n$$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054900 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534644 true] [17592186054900 1100 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A = {x ∈ Z: - 2 \\le x < 6}$ and $B = {x ∈ N: x + 1 < 6}$?\n\n1. $∅⊆B$\n2. $B⊆A$\n3. $A⊈B$" 13194139534644 false] [17592186054900 1100 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186054900 1145 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534644 true] [17592186055155 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534644 true] [17592186055155 1100 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A = {{1}, {2, 3}, {1, 3, 6}}$?\n\n1. ${2, {1}}$\n2. ${{2, 3}}$\n3. ${{-1}, {1, 3, 6}}$" 13194139534644 false] [17592186055155 1100 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{1},{2,3},{1,3,6}}?$\n\n- ${2,{1}}$\n- ${{2,3}}$\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186055155 1145 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${2,{1}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{2,3}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${-1}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534644 true] [17592186300887 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300888 13194139534644 true] [17592186300887 1089 "q-104916" 13194139534644 true] [17592186300887 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534644 true] [17592186300887 1100 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{12},{20,22},{6,9,15}}?$\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$\n- ${{20,22}}$\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$" 13194139534644 true] [17592186300887 1113 #uuid "4788deb7-7ef4-5a10-b588-c3a236a88d09" 13194139534644 true] [17592186300887 1145 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $-1\\notin A.\\,\\times $\